While trying to really optimise a piece of my code I noticed the following
oddity; build a loop which calls a function many times, make sure the
compiler can't inline the function by placing it after the point of
calling.
If the function has no parameters and no results then this runs
faster than all other cases (expected).

However if the routine has one parameter and a result
then it runs faster than a similar routine without a parameter!!

This is really unexpected to me; by the look of the code it must be
to do with allocation of space on the stack at a guess - wierd!!

       parameter    no param
 
result   6            6.79
 
no res   5.28         4.53

