Hi David !

> However if the routine has one parameter and a result
> then it runs faster than a similar routine without a parameter!!
> 
> This is really unexpected to me; by the look of the code it must be
> to do with allocation of space on the stack at a guess - wierd!!

No, not stack at all. Up to six parameters will be passed by registers.
I saw the same effect two months ago.
The funny thing is: 

                void func(void)      int func(int)
caller-loop                   6                  7   instructions
func                          6                  7   instructions

The difference is in the caller loop:

Without args, we have the following scheme:

LABEL:  jsr $26, func
        ...
        ...
        ...
        bne $1, LABEL

This means two branches in two instruction cycles (first the 
conditional and then the subroutine branch.) This is IMHO not 
really optimal.

The version with a dummy arg starts with "bis $31, $31, $16" in LABEL
everything else is the same.

This is the original loop by egcs for calling void a(void); in a loop

	.align 5
$L4:
	jsr $26,a
	ldgp $29,0($26)
	bis $9,$9,$1
	cmple $1,$10,$1
	addl $9,1,$9
	bne $1,$L4

This is !!!slower!!! than

	.align 5
$L4:
	nop
	jsr $26,a
	ldgp $29,0($26)
	bis $9,$9,$1
	cmple $1,$10,$1
	addl $9,1,$9
	bne $1,$L4

I've only added the famous "nop".

I hope Richard Henderson reads this, too ;-}}


  Stefan Traby

