The addresses reported by the kernel seem to be different from what I see
with gdb.  E.g., the program uac.c:

main()
{
        int i, j;
        char *b, buf[10];

        for (i = 0; i < 4; i++) {
                b = &buf[i];
                printf("b = 0x%016lX\n", b);
                j = *((int *)b);
        }
}

produces output

b = 0x000000011FFFFBD8
b = 0x000000011FFFFBD9
b = 0x000000011FFFFBDA
b = 0x000000011FFFFBDB

and kernel messages
unaligned trap at 000000000000001e: 000000011ffffbd9 22 1
unaligned trap at 000000000000001e: 000000011ffffbda 22 1
unaligned trap at 000000000000001e: 000000011ffffbdb 22 1

but gdb reports that main looks like this (gcc -g uac.c -o uac):

0x120000700 <main>:             27bb0011  ldah       gp,17(t12)
0x120000704 <main+4>:           23bd8180  lda        gp,-32384(gp)
0x120000708 <main+8>:           23deffd0  lda        sp,-48(sp)
0x12000070c <main+12>:          b75e0000  stq        ra,0(sp)
0x120000710 <main+16>:          b5fe0008  stq        fp,8(sp)
0x120000714 <main+20>:          47de040f  mov        sp,fp
...
0x120000784 <main+132>:         6bfa8001  ret        zero,(ra),0x1

That is, the data addresses are correct, but where is that 0x1e coming
from?

P.S.  My X server says:
X(4158): unaligned trap at 0000000000000024: 00000001205ad80f 28 2

The PC of 0x24 is clearly junk in this case.

