>>> "B. James Phillippe" said:
> 
> 	I'm running 2.0.35pre6 with the Alpha patches from
> ftp.uk.linux.org.  My system is Avanti (AS 200 4/233).  I have determined
> that if I compile with CONFIG_ALPHA_SRM_SETUP, the de4x5 driver does not
> work.  It detects my card (a DEC 21040) but with the wrong IRQ and IO
> address.  The card is always detected at 0x8000 and IRQ15 normally (and
> works like a champ), but with ...SRM_SETUP it is found at 0x10100 and IRQ5.
> Perhaps I need to better understand the purpose of SRM_SETUP.  I could find
> no documentation (I know, I know, I should read /usr/linux/arch/alpha...)

The intention of CONFIG_ALPHA_SRM_SETUP was to preserve, as much as
possible, the PCI bus configuration settings of the SRM console, instead of
letting the LINUX kernel set them, as is normally done.

The purpose of this was to see if it would allow "graceful" rebooting for
kernels booted directly from SRM console.

Our thinking was that perhaps the reason that a reboot would get "stuck"
between exiting the kernel and re-booting via SRM was that the PCI settings
had been changed and the devices/IRQs would no longer work as SRM expected.

This does seem to work on some platforms, but not (as well) on others, which
is one reason why you have to choose the "Experimental" stuff in order to be
asked the question. Another reason is that it is untested on many of the
Alpha platforms, including AS200.

If you're not concerned with "graceful" reboot, or not willing to be a test
case and perhaps have to do some debugging, don't choose this option.

