[...]
> 
> Methinks that a "suggestion" from a company whose sales of Linux are
> supposed to top 400,000 this year (i.e. RedHat) is far more likely to be
> heard than an individual or company that buys maybe 400 units a year. If
> RedHat chose to do so, they could elect to only bundle the commercial
> software that was available across their (RedHat's) product line, that
> being Intel, Alpha, and Sparc. So far, they have not chosen to do so.
> Their, and our, loss....:(


	And how much do you think Red Hat pays the ISV for each copy of 
bundled software?  (Here's a hint for you, Red Hat 2.1 had *0* bundled apps
and sold for $49.95, Red Hat 5.0 had *3* bundled apps and sold for $49.95)
Now considering that these are *retail* prices, and a huge chunk of Red Hat
sales comes from distribution, you can safely assume that the average 
selling price Red Hat sees is closer to $30 than $50.  Now recalculate
your estimated return for the ISV.   

Now, if 1/10 of the folks who purchased Red Hat would also purchase the new 
Schlepco 9000 Word Processor and Oven Cleaner from Sam's Software Sensations 
*that* would have a greater impact on the potential for the software being 
ported
than Red Hat adopting an "all or none" mentality.  More often than not
that will get the "none" instead of the "all."

Yes, more apps for all flavors of Linux would be a Good Thing (TM).  But
Applixware on Intel is better than no Applixware at all.  Small steps 
at first, and eventually the momentum of ISV support will be self-perpetuating.
Not today, not tommorow, but given a proven platform more people will 
join the club for fear of being left alone in the "MS only" camp.

--Kit


