as I was kicked from it a couple of days ago. Here it is again, anyway.

---------

On Wed, 8 Jul 1998, Mike Sullivan wrote:

> I have a 533MHZ based SX alpha running redhat 5.0 The IDE performance of
> this machine is very poor. Is this a know problem and if so is there 
> a work around ( besides going scsi )

Well, I made a driver for the EIDE part of the cypress chip back in April.
Unfortunately there was a problem with the chip hogging PCI much more than
it should when using block mode transfers, so my driver may have side
effects on your system. My most clear problem is that my sound card can't
play music correctly when doing ide transfers in block mode. Also, when
doing things in parallell over the pci bus, transfer speeds may change
dramatically.

Anyway I might as well share the things I've done in case anyone wants to
experiment with it. Here are my results with an IBM Deskstar 5, 6.4GB.
Speeds was measure with dd sequential reads on 128MB of data:

Mode            Time    Timing params   Transfer speed  Bus speed	Cpu%
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
PIO Mode 0:     61 sek  2/5/12 = 23     2.20 MB/s       1.43MHz		75%
PIO Mode 1:     47 sek  1/4/6  = 14     2.86 MB/s       2.36MHz		80%
PIO Mode 2:     42 sek  1/3/3  = 10     3.20 MB/s       3.33MHz		86%
PIO Mode 3:     39 sek  1/2/2  = 8      3.44 MB/s       4.13MHz		70%
PIO Mode 4:     45 sek  0/2/0  = 5      2.98 MB/s       6.67MHz		70%

DMA MMode 0:    44 sek                  3.05 MB/s                       5%
DMA MMode 1:    20 sek                  6.71 MB/s			12%
DMA MMode 2:    20 sek                  6.71 MB/s			12%

DMA SMode 0:    90 sek                  1.49 MB/s			3.5%
DMA SMode 1:    73 sek                  1.84 MB/s			3.5%
DMA SMode 2:    65 sek                  2.06 MB/s			2.5%

I have attached my patch (for linux-2.0.32) and a small program for
setting DMA mode for both channels (I'm not sure if hdparm can set the
mode correctly, but I know my program does).


Any feedback on how to solve the PCI problems is welcome. Right now I see
now other solution but to divide the transfers into smaller ones, and I
don't like that solution. As it is now, ide transfers may tie up the PCI
bus while transferring 64kB of data, and this is unacceptable.

I tried to force the PCI chipset to break up too large transfers, and this
worked, but then I X started to lock up (Matrox Millennium) instead :(

/Andreas


