Sigh;
	As the original poster of this thread, I almost feel as though I
owe RedHat an apology for form it has taken.
	Just to get this back on track:
	My original request (to RedHat) was to offer an option to order
the installation media for multiple platforms when ordering the boxed
set. I have more than one platform at home (for various reasons), and
when ordering a new version of the OS, it requires that I can either
order the boxed set for one, and have to download the other, "OR", order
two boxed sets, or...or...or... but there is no option to order a boxed
set and get more than one platform included. (please read all of this
before doing the usual, answer it in pieces routine!)
	I suggested to them that when ordering "on-line" as I always do,
to simply add a check box for additional platforms (one for Alpha, one
for Intel, one for Sparc, etc...), and for a nominal fee, they would
include the installation CD for the extra requested platform. This would
ease the financial burden of having to buy a boxed set for each
platform.
	It was suggested "by RedHat" that the solution to this was to
order the Powertools CD's. This is NOT a solution for the following
reason:
	I order usually order the boxed sets for the Intel because of
the included commercial software.  The problem is that if I order the
Boxed set for the Intel, I would still have to purchase the powertools
set (something I rarely do) just to be able to upgrade the Alpha. This
offers me little in the way of savings over just buying two boxed sets!
	I would gladly accept the versions of the CD from the powertools
set for the appropriate platform (JUST the install CD for the
appropriate platform, NOT the entire powertools set!)
	Thus it would be very simple for RedHat to put a couple of
check-boxes on the order page to request that they include the GPL
version of the CD for an Alpha with your order for an Intel Boxed set. I
would even gladly pay a nominal fee ($5 or so) to offset their costs in
doing this. It would be a good showing on their part, and offer those of
us with multi-platform installations at home a financially easier way to
keep our boxes concurrent.

