On Tue, Jul 14, 1998 at 01:03:37PM -0400, Eric D Prescott wrote:
> I have been given an old apollo monitor model number 008398-001
> config  #002. Being that this is a 19" 1024x800  monitor I want to use it
> on my UDB 166mhz wirh alpha 21066 processor. This monitor has RGB inputs 
> sync on green.  50.519 khz Horizontal freq,  60.0 kHz Vertical freq.

Should read 60Hz not kHz, I guess?

> I have read through the apollo faq and the "monitor-info" file thery were
> of some help but quite enoughf.
> 
> 
> Has anyone had any success with various fixed freq cards with this
> machine? 

You don't really need to use fixed freq cards. You can just use standard
SVGA cards (S3, MGA, etc.) and use SVGATextMode to set the frequency your
monitor desires.
# 128x50 / 1024x800 @ 50.519 kHz hsync, 60.0 Hz vsync
"128x50"  64.26  1024 1040 1152 1272  800 804 812 842  8x16
# 116x48 /  928x768 @ ...
"116x48"  59.76   928  944 1048 1160  768 780 790 842  9x16
could be appropriate SVGATextMode- (and XF86Config-) lines.

Check if your VGA is able to do 65/60 MHz in Text Mode ...
MGA Mill is, as well as some S3 chips.
Check SVGATextMode docu.
And, yes, it runs on Linux-Alpha.

