custumers not what they want !)

Don't get me wrong - I like RedHat - RPM was really a revolution,
but shipping a system with a broken "man" means not raising a new 
standard to me.

I know how complex and hard it is create and maintain such a nice, good 
and powerful distribution and it's really impossible to avoid 
bugs/problems.
But everybody who knows that should careful avoid the word "standard";
it's not only used as a marketing term here, it means "monopoly" too.

There are a lot of things on the wish-list for a "standard" RH system:
(I'm speaking also for our custumers here) 
Just to name some of them:

 o KDE
 o CD-Writing support (maybe xcdrecord)
 o Scanner support (maybe sane)
 o Useful printfilters (maybe apsfilter)
 o ISDN support (HiSax, look at S.u.S.e, a must have in Europe)
 o additional layer for RPM to perform commands on multiple
   machines. (rpm -i -G workgroup package.rpm; workgroup definitions
   in rpmrc; add a networked diff too, a global database able to handle 
   multiple configurations (systems) with replication and journalling
   support, delayed updates for machines not up at moment.)
   We have a big custumer wanting this. There is a lot to do for
   the rpm authors ;-}
 o rm -rf glint; replace it by a usable thing.
 o control-panel replacement (like rpm enhancement, must be able to
   update network-wide, journalling (rcs/cvs ?) support for configuration
   files, not only mv to .rpmorig)
 o A X11 web-browser (axp)


RedHat is really my favorite distribution but it's not complete
nor a standard, sorry.

