      It looks like I'll have to add to this one.


    I've been running redhat ever since a guy at pacific hitech named Peter
    Bowen sent me e-mail regarding my angst over getting an old slackware
    install on a a 386-turned-486 running again. It was right around the time
    that everything was just moving towards elf, and I was torn between
    upgrading slack 1.2 (or whatever it was that came with kernel 0.99pl14) to
    elf or just starting from scratch with a new distribution. So I tried
    redhat 2.0, liked it very much, and stuck with it. I was working for an
    ISP at the time that was just starting up, and I had been following alot
    of the comp.os.linux.* groups religiously. When DEC dumped a whole bunch
    of Multia machines on the market (right around redhat 3.0) we decided to try
    out linux for alpha on the basis that everything needed for an ISP seemed
    to work at the time under alpha linux (I was the test case who bought an
    alpha machine for the hell of it to play with).
      I respect redhat for their attempts and frequent successes at getting
    one hell of a distribution in order. I've tried craftworks, and quite 
    frankly, their support is nonexistent and their distribution is worthless.
    Redhat _has_ had successful distributions in the past (including 5.1 imho)
    and I'm sure that they will in the future. Now come the hard truths.

      Point number 1. Redhat is a company.

    Companies are created to cash in on an idea. Sometimes this idea becomes
    fruitful and yields a bountiful crop of cash, and at other times...well, it
    just doesn't. When people are making money they can pay on their mortgaged 
    homes, buy clothes and candy for their wives and children, and pay their
    banks/dealers/loan sharks off for any borrowed money or other items. It's
    nice to make money and not have to worry about how you are going to pay
    next month's rent, eat, etc. In other words, redhat is out to make money.
    Period. That's what companies are for.

      Point number 2. Redhat is making money off of linux.

    Redhat is making money off of their variant of linux. By variant I mean that
    all of the basic linux framework is there...the GNU copyleft, source code, 
    and so on. However, redhat spiffed things up with an upgrade system that
    usually works, an (almost) idiot proof system set-up, an easily configurable
    X Window system, and a different filesystem structure. For the most part,
    these are all incredibly good ideas. And yet many people I know curse them.
    "It makes linux too easy. It's too cushy. A drooling idiot could use it. 
    Warez puppies and hackers are ruining linux's rep because of it." Okay.
    These both are and aren't valid arguments; sure, it makes linux cushy and
    easy to install. But for those of us that ARE competent unix users, it makes
    things just a little smoother, assuming that you can avoid the lazy factor.
    But the main point was that redhat is making money off of this. It's 
    fighting with slackware, debian, suse, and the bazillions of other variants
    of linux out there. And since it's a company, in MY opinion it should be 
    held to the same rules that companies are held to. What does this mean?
      1. If you don't buy the product, then don't expect support for it. If you
    have a unique and wierd thing that occurs that we have never seen before, 
    we might help out of pure interest. But don't hold your breath.
      2. If you DO buy the product, we shall promptly (read: as soon as 
    humanly possible) help you with any issues that you have, within reason. 
    In other words, don't expect us to teach you unix, but if you have a 
    legitimate problem then we will help. I've personally watched friends
    grow old and die waiting for nonexistent support from redhat.
      3. If we release shit, then we will gladly deshit your system for free.
    In other words, if we send out a product to you that says that it will 
    boot on your machine and it doesn't because we screwed the pooch, then 
    we will happily send you a fix in the mail for free. Because we understand
    that you may not be running our product to play xquake, but to do molecular
    modeling or other serious work. But for the most part, this won't happen
    because we will test our software before we ship it. This is called 
    accountability, and we agree to it. We might even think about sending out 
    a periodic updates CD for free to paying customers because we screwed 
    up so bad.

      Point number 3. Alpha Linux just doesn't sell (yet)
      
      Suprise. It doesn't. And this is probably redhat's fault. 
      Out of all of the released distributions, I have only purchased 2 of them,
    either directly or indirectly from redhat. And both had some serious quality
    control issues. Only one would install on my system, and the other one 
    required a week or so wait for a fix for my machine to install. Since these
    events, I have refused to purchase a redhat product until I see that their
    quality control has improved. Redhat 5.1 MIGHT be the starting point for 
    this, but I'm hoping that it's not a too little/too late issue. Redhat 5.0
    was so hopelessly broken out of the box that I almost ran back to 4.2 
    immediately. This may be beating a dead horse with a stick, but the gist
    is the following:

       Dear redhat people.
     
       You aren't big enough to act like microsoft yet. Please fix the broken
       things in your distribution before you sell it to someone. You are 
       representing the linux world because you are popular, but don't confuse
       popularity with quality. I understand that many of the broken things 
       are not your fault, but please please PLEASE release quality product. 
       I'd be willing to wait an extra month and I would buy your product for
       more than what you are asking for it if I could be guaranteed that your 
       product would run well. You guys have done good a few times in the 
       past. What happened?
       
    But this will probably never happen, as there just isn't the demand for 
    alpha stuff that there is for intel. We don't have a complete web browser
    yet, nor do we have the surplus of commercial apps that intel linux does. 
    Therefore, there aren't the same bells and whistles for the alpha people.
    Most of the alpha people have a clue. This means that people will simply 
    flock to the distribution that is more robust in terms of packages and 
    stability. Were I in redhat's shoes, this is what I would do.

      1. Improve my customer relations. Answer e-mail promptly, without a 
      form letter if possible. Let people know where they are in the 
      backlog queue. 

      2. Test, test test. Improve my quality control. I'm in it for the money,
      so I damn well better release good stuff.

      3. Once I have everything running better, port more packages. Offer more
      flexibility. This is where I'll save my ass, because there just isn't the
      surplus of alpha ports for serious apps YET. 

   Okay, enough of this rant. I have research to do. Redhat guys, I really do
   want to stick with you. Please keep your noses clean.

     Eric.


