Then you haven't been looking at the archives for this mailing list back very
far.  There is probably a complaint about this every week or so.

syslogd, after some hours or days of operating, will suddenly start consuming
100% of the CPU time of the processor, and spitting out the follwing into
/var/log/messages:

Jul 16 15:39:02 canoe syslogd: select: Bad file descriptor
Jul 16 15:39:33 canoe last message repeated 32511 times
Jul 16 15:40:34 canoe last message repeated 79918 times
Jul 16 15:41:35 canoe last message repeated 76453 times
Jul 16 15:42:36 canoe last message repeated 76384 times
Jul 16 15:43:37 canoe last message repeated 81842 times
Jul 16 15:44:38 canoe last message repeated 84954 times
Jul 16 15:45:39 canoe last message repeated 80640 times
Jul 16 15:46:40 canoe last message repeated 72752 times
Jul 16 15:47:41 canoe last message repeated 73687 times
Jul 16 15:48:42 canoe last message repeated 67014 times
Jul 16 15:49:43 canoe last message repeated 57358 times
Jul 16 15:50:43 canoe last message repeated 75333 times
Jul 16 15:51:42 canoe last message repeated 76780 times

The only solution is to kill syslogd.  Various people have removed it from the
runlevel startup directories so that it doesn't start up.  Apparantly you can
postpone the inevitable by increasing the number of file descriptors.

