> message saying hit a key to return to Arc and it simply goes back again,
>    Can anybody help me ? I have read through all the docs etc that I can find and
> I can't find anything...as they all seem to asume that getting as far as the Milo
> prompt should be easy, What am I doing wrong...?

I went through the exact same blues in the past days.
Your problem is the boot partition on the hard disk, even if the
firmware does not give any clue in this sense.
It must be a bug in ARCS BIOS. It appears to be impossible to construct
a valid boot partition using Linux tools (fdisk, mkdosfs, dd etc)
on that machine.  You have to use ARCINST from the Windows NT CDROM.

It may seem absurd but this is what I had to do, basically
(of course this is just a summary not including wrong paths..):

1) follow http://www.deskstation.com/linux/install.html
   to get Linux running (but use the new MILO from gatekeeper.dec.com
   instead of the one from Deskstation)
   The RH installation made you go through fdisk, you wrote the
   partition table on the disk.  You dutifully made a FAT partition
   on sda1: it is unusable as it is, do not bother to copy
   msdos files over it, not even from ARCS BIOS.  The files go there,
   you see them, but the system will not boot from them.
2) run ARCINST, delete and recreate the boot partition over the
   same boundaries
3) go in ARCS and copy the three MSDOS files into this partition
4) now you should be able to boot MILO.   From there
   boot Linux again.  fdisk will complain about partition 1
   (logical sectors do not match physical sectors, or something
   like that), and the swap partition also gives errors
   (mkswap cannot format it).   Congratulations: by running
   ARCINST you have damaged the rest of the disk!  But
   you can still do dd if=/dev/sda1 of=/boot.img and save
   your boot partition in a file.
   Go in fdisk and rewrite the partition table to make it
   Linux-friendly.  You have now destroyed again your boot
   partition!  But you can restore its contents:
      dd if=/boot.img of=/dev/sda1
   Now both ARCS BIOS and fdisk are happy with it: do not
   touch it any more.
5) Reboot and reinstall Linux all over again, skipping
   the fdisk stage.

It's easy! ;-)

I can made you available our  "boot.img", but I am not sure
if it is disk-dependent or not.  I have a Fujitsu MAB3045.

Hope it helps

