On Fri, 17 Jul 1998, Ryan Kirkpatrick wrote:

> 	I am looking at buying an XL300 system in the near future to
> replace/augment an aging UDB. 

We've purchased a few of these, and I'm happy to put in my $.02.

Ross A. Knepper mentioned the nice design of these things.  I too was
impressed by the inside of the box.  Looks very solid, with nice extra's
like an active terminator at the end of the internal scsi cable.  I
found this out right away because machine #1 didn't work out of the box.
Turned out the power wasn't connected to the motherboard.  Plugged it
in, and everything worked fine...except that the external SCSI jack was
a nop.  Okay, this one goes back.  Machine #2, the floppy drive didn't
work at first...turned out the drive cable wasn't fully plugged in.
Fixed that, no more problems.  Machine #3 and replacement for #1 worked
first time, no problems.

I'm not sure who's to blame for the loose cables.  I don't believe
either could have come loose in shipping (they go in quite firmly), and
my retailer claims they didn't even open the boxes on most of these
units.  Anyway, for the price we paid this is not something I'm going to
complain about. ;^)

Ross also mentioned that these are "XLT"'s.  This is a good tip.

Jaap Schellekens complained a little about the noise.  I guess this is
relative.  They are quieter than most of the other boxes I have around
here, but you know when they are on.

Concerning memory, I've got 96 MB in mine. 4 8's and 4 16's.  Which
reminds me...you have to remove the cpu card to get to the memory
slots.  This is explained in the detailed hardware booklet.  The
documentation is nicely done.

I had absolutely no problem installing Linux.

In summary, we had a few initial problems, but after that smooth
sailing.  I really like these boxes a lot, and think they are a great
bargain.  If I had it to do all over again, I would.

-mike

