roman@songdog.eskimo.com (Bill Roman)
> Rumor from a usually reliable source (i.e., secondhand from hardware
> engineering at DEC) has it that Avantis require *exactly* 70 nsec. RAM.
> Not slower, not faster.  This could be the source of the problem.

If that's true (which I doubt), I would NEVER EVEN CONSIDER buying an Avantis.

A proper memory controller makes NO assumptions about what the output of
the memory is before the access time is elapsed.  It could be correct data,
or it could be trash, but until the access time has elapsed it doesn't
matter.

In point of fact, 70 nS memory is usually capable of operating at 60 nS.
But it hasn't been tested by the manufacturer for 60 nS.  Or (much less
common) it has been tested at 60 nS and failed.  Also, memory that works at
70 nS over the full rated operating temperature range can be expected to be
somewhat faster than 70 nS at typical operating temperatures.

Note that the access time specification on DRAM is a maximum.  There is
never any guarantee that the data won't arrive sooner.

Any hardware design that does not take this into acount is unaceptable, and
the engineer of such a brain-damaged design should be fired.

I seriously doubt that there is actually any truth to this rumor; DEC usually
has pretty good engineers.

By the way, this is the same reason that the stories about not mixing different
speeds of memory are bogus.  As long as all the memory is at least as fast as
it is required to be, it won't hurt for some of it to be faster.

Cheers,
Eric


