On Fri, 17 Jul 1998, Paul Tomblin wrote:

> Quoting furio ercolessi (furio@spin.it):
> > > 
> > > syslogd, after some hours or days of operating, will suddenly start consuming
> > > 100% of the CPU time of the processor, and spitting out the follwing into
> > > /var/log/messages:
> > > 
> > > Jul 16 15:39:02 canoe syslogd: select: Bad file descriptor
> > > Jul 16 15:39:33 canoe last message repeated 32511 times
> > 
> > We also had this problem, with RH5.1 and a 2.0.30 kernel.
> > This prevented us from putting the machine into production.
> > Yesterday I finally managed to compile a working 2.0.34
> > for our platform, and the problem has not reappeared so far.
> > Of course syslogd itself remained the same, so the problem may
> > well be in the kernel.
> 
> The machine this happened to had been running for 7 days when it happened.
> RedHat 5.0 and 2.0.34.  It's happened many times in the past without changing
> anything.  All rpms installed are updated from the RedHat ftp site.
> 
> The machine wasn't under heavy load.  Just the usual 10 or 20 sendmail
> processes, the usual 5 innxmits, innd, a couple of newsx news slurpers, a few
> telnet sessions.


This potentially could be because of bug in glibc preventing select on >32
fds(grr). Try upgrading to latest glibc, and it may solve the problem. 

-alex


