I now managed to induce the syslogd/klogd syndrome on
my system (Ruffian, RH5.1, kernel 2.0.34) by running 'ntpdate':

# ntpdate 123.123.123.123
19 Jul 21:01:50 ntpdate[6741]: poll() error: Operation not permitted
19 Jul 21:01:50 ntpdate[6741]: poll() error: Operation not permitted
[....]
(until ^C pressed)

In the logs:

Jul 19 21:01:50 foo kernel: Cannot read proc file system: 9 - Bad file descriptor. 
Jul 19 21:02:20 foo last message repeated 265385 times
[...]

At this point syslogd and klogd are eating all the CPU as usual. 
Killing and restarting them does not help: the new copies still
eat CPU. This seems to suggest that the problems are elsewhere.
I used

# ls -l /usr/sbin/ntpdate
-rwxr-xr-x   1 root     root       111896 May 10 22:54 /usr/sbin/ntpdate

from xntp3-5.93-2.alpha.rpm (I do not remember where I grabbed this,
I cannot find a source right now).

The original xntp 3-5.93 does not compile out of the box on alpha Linux.

furio ercolessi
spin - trieste (italy)


