> > This implies ramdom behavior. Not good. If you're only getting
> > unaligned traps, that would not be random, I would think.
> > Take your memory out and see if it still does these things.
> > If it does, it's probably SW. If it works consistently, I'd
> > bet you have a DIMM problem.
> 
> If I take the new memory out things are happy.
> 
> > >     3) **** This is the killer! I wrote this little memory test (see
> > >        below); it hit a mismatch last night in which one byte of the
> > >        stored value read back as 'ff' but should have been something
> > >        else.  There were no CIA errors etc (I've not had any at all)
> > >        How is this possible? Is this a RAM fault - but if there were no
> > >        CIA errors isn't it more likely its an OS error overwriting
> > >        something? Its done over 60 loops of this today and had no
> > >        problems.
> >
> > Run with a smaller array on the old memory and see if it's
> > stable as above.
> 
> I've taken the new RAM out and all seems happy. What I don't understand
> is that if its a hardware fault why the system isn't complaining?
> Especially if its ECC then it should be shouting at me when ever it has to
> do a correction and it should presumably detect a lot of the errors
> even if it can't fix them.
> 
> Also it only does it under some conditions (its a lot less happy with X
> running - especially X11perf...).
> 
> Could the X server be corrupting things (or the graphics card) - I've
> turned the PCI parity on and am also not seeing any problems.
> 

Don't know if the Xserver can cause problems for memory.
Seen it trash my network card (from other thread) but not
memory. Even ECC can have undetected errors. I believe
ECC can do 1 bit correction, two bit detection. 3 bit
errors may or may not be caught. True parity does only
1 bit detection, and non-parity lets anything go...
If you're getting that many bit errors, maybe your
DIMM's are out of spec??

For reference, I use 10ns SDRAM, 3.3v, non-buffered, 8x72 (64MB ea)


Wes


