I'm just quoting from memory. I think it's 8 bits per 8 bytes,
(x64 vs x72) Pretty sure about ECC's ability to only correct
1 bit and detect two. Since each bit is usually on a different
chip, that would imply the DIMM's were really out of wack if
three at once had problems. Point is just that ECC or True
parity does not mean you're guaranteed the data is correct
in memory. Bad memory is bad memory. Also, I think True parity
uses 1 bit per byte, ECC uses 2 bits per byte which leads me
to believe x72 DIMM's are True parity, not ECC. Any HW types
know for sure?


Wes


