I've no idea how long this has been there -- probably since you
wrote the thing in the first place.  But I've just spent nigh on
to 5 days tracking down a bit of random corruption that I'd
believed to be caused by a broken compiler.


--- include/asm-alpha/page.h.orig	Mon Oct 14 01:51:59 1996
+++ include/asm-alpha/page.h	Sun Jul 19 22:48:16 1998
@@ -32,7 +32,7 @@ static inline void clear_page(unsigned l
 		"stq $31,40(%1)\n\t"
 		"stq $31,48(%1)\n\t"
 		"stq $31,56(%1)\n\t"
-		"addq $1,64,$1\n\t"
+		"addq %1,64,%1\n\t"
 		"bne %0,1b"
 		:"=r" (count),"=r" (page)
 		:"0" (PAGE_SIZE/64), "1" (page));


We should seriously consider getting rid of these two asm blocks 
altogether.  Yes, all released versions of gcc will do stupid
things with that simple loop, but I've been hacking the shit
out of loop.c for a few months, and my patches don't do stupid
things with this. 

And it's not like older versions produce incorrect code...


r~



--- include/asm-alpha/page.h.orig	Mon Oct 14 01:51:59 1996
+++ include/asm-alpha/page.h	Sun Jul 19 22:54:22 1998
@@ -19,54 +19,51 @@
  */
 static inline void clear_page(unsigned long page)
 {
-	unsigned long count;
-	__asm__ __volatile__(
-		".align 4\n"
-		"1:\n\t"
-		"stq $31,0(%1)\n\t"
-		"stq $31,8(%1)\n\t"
-		"stq $31,16(%1)\n\t"
