On 20 Jul 1998 SMS@provis.com wrote:

>    Greetings:
> 
>    I have one of those Onsale bargain AlphaStation 200 4/233 systems and
> the Red Hat 5.1 CD kit, and a few (probably lame) questions.

Welcome to the Onsale AS/200 club.  How many of us are there, anyway?

>    1.  I wish to use the SRM console.  What do I need to do in the hard
> disk (Seagate ST15230WC, another Onsale bargain) partitioning (using
> "fdisk", I presume) to make it possible to boot from the hard disk with
> no floppy involved?

Here is what I did:

1.) Start the RedHat install.
2.) Partition using fdisk, leaving space at the beginning of the drive (1MB
should be fine, but you may be forced to waste more due to cylinder-size).
Write down the offsets you used for each partition.  Save the changes.
3.) *re*-partition, this time using BSD disklabels (the 'b' option in
fdisk).  Create the same partitions, including the beginning space, using
the same offsets.  This is to work around a problem in the RedHat install.
4.) Procede with the install.
5.) After install is complete and you've booted off the floppy, run:
	swriteboot /dev/sda bootlx (assuming your root partition is /dev/sda2).
6.) Reboot.
7.) In the SRM console, you now want to use:
	b dka0 -fi 2/vmlinux.gz -fl "root=/dev/sda2"  (you can set these as
default in the SRM console).

>    3.  I currently have a fixed-rate monitor which the DEC video card
> (ZLXp-E2, PBXGA-BA) likes just fine (switch setting 2, 1280x1024, 60Hz). 
> The Red Hat installation program (kernel?) quickly buggers this,
> requiring a PC-style VGA monitor.  Is there any way to get Linux to
> leave the video board alone so I can use my chosen monitor for all
> purposes?

Ouch, that's not one I know how to help you with.  You *might* be able to
specify a video mode in the os_flags (-fl boot command line) setting that
works at that resolution.  I think that might be your problem.

>    Feel free to point me to an info reservoir, if all the answers are
> already recorded somewhere.

Did you see the Alpha-HOWTO and/or SRM HOWTO?  This and much more can be
found at http://www.azstarnet.com/~axplinux (dig around a bit).

>    Considering all the wonderful things I've been hearing about Linux
> for a commercial environment, I've been surprised by how much trouble
> I've had getting this stuff going.  (The "man" failure was pretty

I agree it's an uphill battle, but there is a plateau, and once you reach
it, I think you'll find it worth the effort.  BTW, your next big hurdle
will be X.  Fortunately, Tim Rowley has graced us with some patches to
XF86_TGA that get it working, abeit a bit slow.

Hope this helps,
