> 
>   bob.f *= 2;
> 
>   printf ("The value is %g.\n", bob.f );
> }
> 
> This program crashes when I multiply a denorm by 2. Is it the case
> under Linux/Alpha that denorms should never be generated and cause fp
> exceptions when you do any operations with them?
> 

The HW manual I looked at says this is not maskable. It
will always interrupt. The more common case is a FP
array being overwritten by something non-FP and then
attempting to do FP ops on it, or a non-initialized stack
variable being used. All cases I've found in my
code and others this was a bug in the code, not an
intended calculation. Intel seems to just treat this as
a real small number, effectively zero, so it has little
effect on the FP op, except maybe if you try divide by one.

I think Uncle George had a case where he thought it
was a valid calculation but I haven't.

A bug is a bug. If it were my code, I'd just fix it.
Only other choice is to slow down the code with -mieee.


Wes


