I managed to do something last night and this morning that I rather wish I
couldn't do - I lockup up my UDB box tight as a safe and produced a spew
of error messages on the console. I was performing a cpio driven write to
a scsi zip drive, the load average started climbing, and the errors showed
up when I wasn't looking (I was telnetted in anyway) The scsi errors
appeared to originate with the scsi driver, but I cleverly failed to write
them down 8-( <<dumb>>.

Let me restart with a description of the setup. My UDB has its internal
HD with Milo & swap on it, and an external device chain consisting of a
1gig HD (/dev/sdb1), a tape drive, a cdrom and the zip drive (/dev/sdc1).
I have the /home directory nsf mounted from my other linux box (intel).

I was telnetted in from the intel to the UDB (ssh actually) and fiddling
with getting a personal backup script to have a few saftey features (I'm
not a programmer, so I need to work on my error checks when scripting) as
I wrote my personal disk hierarchy to the zip disk.

(Basically, I wanted to make a single command line safe for myself)
The command I executed was
find . -name cache -prune -or print | cpio -padm /mnt/zip/tfreeman
from /home/tfreeman. This worked for 2 minutes or less, when top reported
that the load average was climbing (I think it hit 1.7 or so) before
everything locked up. Error messages were on the console, but I don't know
if they were still scrolling. Content of the messages was to the effect
that the scsi device driver could not access a (varying?) block on the
disk.

I fsck'd all the disks on reboot (lots of errors on the zip), and retried
but added another window su'd to root. When the error messages are
displayed, the root process can not access anything on the hard disk, if
it isn't locked up solid itself. (I tried this three or four times, but I
wasn't counting)

The work around was to recreate the fs on the zip disk from scratch, and
things work just fine.

Is this properly considered a bug, and if so, who best to get it?

My thanks to the list in advance.

