OK, I'd better set the record straight:

0. the appearance of these messages coincides with the use of the kernels
   or MILOs I generated for the Red Hat 5.1 release, as well as any kernels
   or MILOs gerneated by the use of the alpha-patches-2.0.34-0.1.

1. the message is strictly a debugging one that I inadvertantly left on
   when I released the alpha-patches-2.0.34-0.1. I will disable it in the
   next set of patches; sorry.

2. the operation of certain drivers in the kernel (serial, IDE, floppy, IIRC),
   depend on the generation of unexpected interrupts in order to determine
   which interrupt their device is actually working on, or they actually
   cause an interrupt to occur before they register their interrupt handler.
   for reference, see the auot-irq stuff in arch/*/kernel/irq.c, IIRC.
   So, when you see unexpected interrupts for IRQs 3, 4, 6, and 14, those
   are those drivers normal probing operations.

3. certain platforms, XXLT and AS500 in particular, have seen the appearance
   of infinite messages; this indicates another mistake I made, in believing
   that an ALCOR kernel or MILO would operate correctly on the XLT or AS500.
   the current ALCOR MILOs and kernels will not; this will be fixed in the
   next round of patches, such that ana ALCOR/XLT/AS500 MILO and kernel will
   dynamically determine which platform is present and "do the right thing".
   
4. other platforms or configurations may see this message as well, under
   conditions which I don't fully understand; Greg Lindahl's case is one of
   them. IRQ 18 for him is the DE500 Ethernet adapter; the message seems to
   indicate that the particular driver chosen for the RH 5.1 PC164 kernel
   was one which has a problem with his particular adapter and/or media type
   (I know others of you have encountered strange behavior with DE500 and
   100Mbit operation in various boxes with the different drivers, especially
   MIATAs). we are investigating this offline.

5. if you wish to "adjust" your kernel sources to disable this message, look
   for arch/alpha/kernel/irq.c:device_interrupt(), and simply change the
   "#if 1" to "#if 0" to prevent its occurance.

Also, if anyone else is seeing *lots* of messages, like Greg, please get in
touch with me, see if we can determine what the cause might be...

--Jay++

