Howdy,
 I believe they use Hamming code to do the error correction.  If not, they use a code with similar properties in terms of protection bits required.  For Hamming code, use the following table:

 bits to protect from single bit errors    protection bits required
 --------------------------------------    ------------------------
               1                                      1
               2                                      2
              3-4                                     3
              5-8                                     4
              9-16                                    5
             17-32                                    6
             33-64                                    7
             65-128                                   8

 As you see, for the 64 bit data width of most newer cpus, a standard parity simm provides enough bits to do the error correction.  That was not true until we got to the 64 bit wide memory accesses.

 I did not find an online reference that I though completely explained this issue, but for some more details look at
  http://www.rad.com/networks/1994/err_con/hamming.htm

Ralph Green, Jr.

