Brett Andrews wrote:
> 
> Wes (and anyone else who might want to know):
> 
> Well, here goes.
> 
> With true parity, the codewords are 9 bits long (8 bits data, 1 bit
> parity).  Because there is only 1 bit of parity, and 1 bit can only have
> two possible states (off or on), then it's kinda like saying there's an
> error or not (but not being able to say in which data bit the error
> occurred, or how to properly decode it).
> 
> With the ECC as in RAM, the codewords are 72 bits long (64 bits data, 8
> bits parity).  With 8 bits of parity, you get 256 different possible states
> for the whole parity block (8 bits).  Since there are a total of 72
> single-bit errors possible with 72-bit codewords, the 256 possible states
> for the parity block are more than enough to properly decode a codeword
> that has a single-bit error (and also detect double-bit errors).  This is
> why ECC is better than true parity, even though overall it uses the same
> number of parity bits per data bit.  If you think about parity in terms of
> bits per codeword, it may make more sense.  I hope this is a good enough
> explanation.  I didn't want to get into syndromes, parity check matrices,
> and maximum likelihood decoding tables....
> 

Thanks, Makes sense to me. The low level detail
isn't an issue to me. I do SW anyway. Just nice
to have some idea how it works.

One other term I've heard was bit steering where
there are extra bits available to be reassigned
when a chip goes bad. Think IBM uses it on their
more expensive equipment.


