Thus spake Wes Bauske (wsb@paralleldata.com):

> Ralph Green, Jr. wrote:
> > 
> > Howdy,
> >  I believe they use Hamming code to do the error correction.  If not, they use a code with similar properties in terms of protection bits required.  For Hamming code, use the following table:
> > 
> >  bits to protect from single bit errors    protection bits required
> >  --------------------------------------    ------------------------
> >                1                                      1
> >                2                                      2
> >               3-4                                     3
> >               5-8                                     4
> >               9-16                                    5
> >              17-32                                    6
> >              33-64                                    7
> >              65-128                                   8

And the reason for these numbers is not actually that difficult to
see:

If there are N data bits and K check bits then we need to be able to
correct a single bit error in (N+K) bits (it's quite possible the
error may affect a check rather than data bit)

The K bits give us 2**K possible bit patterns for the check bits and
the usual SEC-DED (single error correcting, double error detecting)
scheme works as follows:

The original N data bits are passed through a check-bit-generating
function (call it f) which produces K check bits, which are stored
with the data.

On retrieval, the N data bits are passed through function f again. The
regenerated K check bits are then XORed with the retrieved K check
bits to produce what is called the syndrome. 

A 0 syndrome means no error, which leaves exactly 2**K - 1 bit patterns
in the syndrome to identify single bit errors.

Therefore 2**K - 1 >= N + K
