Last week a customer brought in a Alpha clone in my store for the purpose
of reconfiguring OS's and software to his liking.  I was a bit hesitant
because I've no experience with the Alpha platform.  But ( and a big one
at that) I've done quite a bit of work with Micro$oft packages and quite a
bit work work with Linux on Intel Platforms. I currently run four Linux
boxes.

With trepidation in my heart I dove in.  Repartitioned, reinstalled NT4.O
as that was recommeded by the MILO howto.  Pleasantly surprised by the
fact that this probably was the smoothest NT4.0 install I've ever done.
No need to reinstall four times in succession :^)  Then on to Linux.

Man, was partitioning a pain.  Disk Druid (Redhat 5.0 CD install) would
not let me add a third primary partition.  Got so frustrated, that I had
to use fdisk just to get the job done.  After much wailing and gnashing of
teeth, Linux went on and even respected my partitioning requirements.

I would have been happy with the stock kernel, expect parallel port
support was not compiled in.  Sigh. So I took compiling a kernel for this
beastie.  Read the docs.  "mrproper, make xconfig, make dep; make clean"
seems normal enough.  Ahhh, need to "make boot" rather than "make zImage".
Copied the kernel to the root of the disk.
Okay, no problem.  Did a "make modules; make modules_install" seemed to go
well enough.  A few minor errors, hopefully not big enough to stop the
process.

Reconfigured "NT ARC" (is this the right name for the Intel BIOS
equivalent?) to let me boot the new kernel.  Reboot.  The system sees the
kernel in the root partition.  It loads the kernel into memory, it unzips
the kernel, but right after it does the system hangs or at least seems to.
I've let it set there for 15 minute or more with no change in status. 

Hmmm, reboot using the original kernel. System comes up but, again without
parallel support. Sigh.  

Since then, I rebuilt the kernel numerous times with different devices
enabled/disabled.  I went to ftp.kernel.org and downloaded  pristine
sources and the alpha patch from gatekeeper.dec.com.  Did a "rm -Rf
/usr/src/linux" prior to installing the source and patch.  reconfigured
the kernel, and tried to boot on the new kernel.  Same effect.  The kernel
gets loaded but hangs after the unzip process.

Some specs on the system:

Built by ENROREX		URL:www.enorex.com
System board:			PC164
Memory:				128Mb
HD:				Maxtor 4.8Gb Model 85120A8
CDrom:				Generic IDE interace, Manfacturer unknown
CPU:				433Mhz,  not sure about this
NIC:				Ne2000 clone, used supplied by me.
SCSI:				None
Linux:				Redhat 5.0


Hopefully, someone will be able to point me the right direction for the
kernel hanging issue.  Beyond that I've some questions too:

1.	Is milo designed to mate to a particular range of kernel versions
	or is it strictly based on what hardware architecture?

2.	Is NT ARC the same as SRM.  I gathered from the howto and Redhat's
	install book that it is different from Alpha Bios

3.	I'm staying with the 2.0.30 kernal that came with the RedHat CD.
	Should I be using a newer one?


For not having seen or used this hardware, I'm surprised I got it going
as quick as I did.  But minor miracle do happen.

I subscribed to the list last thursday when the customer brought the
system in.  I must say, the chaf level here is significantly lower than
the intel based RedHat mail lists.  Very technical.


Keith


