Well, I can't say about DEC's top end graphics (haven't been following since
the Kubota fiasco), but at their low end it is technologially identical to
the PC world with the exception of software support.  There are some amazing
PCI 3D graphics cards for the PC which may as well be cardboard for all the
good it does Linux users.  Until someone comes out with OpenGL support for
the 3D hardware, there is very little force driving people to work on Alpha
support.

Under NT, there is starting to be some very impressive software available
using the OpenGL drivers (Sculpt comes to mind), but until the video card
rendering engines can be used optimally, the Alpha CPU has to do it all, and
even for an Alpha, that's a lot of work.

Because of their entrenchment and expertise, the high end graphics field
still belongs to SGI.  Their i/o and SMP technology is still tops and
especially their integration of a lot of technology is very smooth.  My 6 yr
old R4K is still competitive with much of the current high end PC
technology, with the exception of pure compute power.  Pity they're going to
NT.

Cheers,
Harry

