Ah, do NOT use "make menuconfig" or "make xconfig", they are not reliable
at this time on the Alphas; use the trusty old "make config"...

> Okay, no problem.  Did a "make modules; make modules_install" seemed to go
> well enough.  A few minor errors, hopefully not big enough to stop the
> process.

Modules should work fine.

> Reconfigured "NT ARC" (is this the right name for the Intel BIOS
> equivalent?) to let me boot the new kernel.

It's really better, for testing purposes, to have a Boot Option that only
starts MILO, then you can tell *it* to boot whichever kernel you want. This
is in addition to a full LINUX boot into the kernel. However, some ARC
consoles are limited WRT the number of Boot Options that one can define, and
since you want LINUX and NT, you may have used up the two that are allowed.

> Reboot.  The system sees the
> kernel in the root partition.  It loads the kernel into memory, it unzips
> the kernel, but right after it does the system hangs or at least seems to.
> I've let it set there for 15 minute or more with no change in status. 

This is not untypical behavior for a kernel built under "make menuconfig",
unfortunately.

> Since then, I rebuilt the kernel numerous times with different devices
> enabled/disabled.  I went to ftp.kernel.org and downloaded  pristine
> sources and the alpha patch from gatekeeper.dec.com.  Did a "rm -Rf
> /usr/src/linux" prior to installing the source and patch.  reconfigured
> the kernel, and tried to boot on the new kernel.  Same effect.  The kernel
> gets loaded but hangs after the unzip process.

An observation:

it is NOT a good idea to replace the /usr/src/linux tree; that has been
specifically tested by Red Hat to ensure building of apps works; a patched
kernel tree, from any other source, may cause problems. I recommend the use
of /usr/local/src as a place to build kernels from other source trees.

> 1.	Is milo designed to mate to a particular range of kernel versions
> 	or is it strictly based on what hardware architecture?

The latest MILOs should boot 2.0.xx and 2.1.xx kernels correctly, if those
are built under RH 4.2 or 5.x with the standard compilation subsystem and
binutils. It is NOT necessary to match MILO version with kernel version,
except when building the MILO.

Look for the latest MILO for PC164 at:

  gatekeeper.dec.com:/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/Test-Milos-2.0.30+/pc164

> 2.	Is NT ARC the same as SRM.  I gathered from the howto and Redhat's
> 	install book that it is different from Alpha Bios

No, it is NOT the same as SRM. It is quite similar to AlphaBIOS, which newer
platforms use instead of ARC. You must boot the correct kernel for the console
that you are running under.

> 3.	I'm staying with the 2.0.30 kernal that came with the RedHat CD.
> 	Should I be using a newer one?

The alpha-sources-2.0.34-0.1.tar.gz that you'll find under the Kernels/
directory (as above) is pretty much the latest stable kernel sources for
Alpha. There are reports that one can patch 2.0.35 with the alpha-patches
for 2.0.34, but there are also reports that some of the drivers enhanced
in 2.0.35 are not (enhanced) on the Alpha, but are problematic.

Good luck.

