I think it's possible that the patches were installed incorrectly; did you
do essentially the following?

	cd /usr/local/src
	tar xzf /somewhere/linux-2.0.34.tar.gz
	mv linux linux-2.0.34
	cd linux-2.0.34
	zcat /somewhere/alpha-patches-2.0.34-0.1.gz | patch -p1 -E >& ../34.log

The most important steps are 4 and 5; you must first "cd" into the top level
of the source tree, and then tell "patch" to remove the first level directory
names from the files in the diffs. If you miss either of these, you are apt
to miss the creation of critical files in the correct places.

> I'd still like to use ARC for booting in order not to need a floppy.
> However, the MILOs produced by Jay do hang on SCSI bus scan. I tried to
> build my own MILOs, but these do hang after displaying (from memory)
> Swapping to PALCode 0x0 ...

You CANNOT build MILOs with the DEC BIOS emulation under anything but RH 4.2,
at this time!!! And building with the free x86 emulator is quite a problem, as
that code is not up to emulating some of the later VGA BIOSes.

I don't know what the MILO problem with the SCSI is, but one can boot directly
from harddisk under SRM console, so long as the harddisk is partitioned using
a BSD-style partition table (SRM does NOT understand DOS style). Unfortunately,
that may mean reinstalling after redoing the disk partitioning... :-\

Good luck.

