On Wed, 22 Jul 1998 Jay.Estabrook@digital.com wrote:

> I don't know what the MILO problem with the SCSI is, but one can boot directly
> from harddisk under SRM console, so long as the harddisk is partitioned using
> a BSD-style partition table (SRM does NOT understand DOS style). Unfortunately,
> that may mean reinstalling after redoing the disk partitioning... :-\

 Oh, come on.  You may change the partitioning scheme from Intel to BSD
on-line using a combination of `fdisk' and `dd'.  Been there, done that.
Just make sure you enter exactly the same numbers for partition offsets
and sizes.

 The exact order is as follows:

1. Read and write down the values displayed by `fdisk'.  Make sure you
have no more than eight real partitions ("extended" does not count as
real).  If you have more than eight partitions then you may ether
sacrifice some of them or use the traditional "backup and restore" method.

2. Quit `fdisk'.

3. Perform `dd if=/dev/zero of=<whole_disk_device> bs=512 count=1'.

4. Start `fdisk' in BSD mode and create a disklabel using values obtained
in step 1.  Omit all extended partitions as they make no sense with BSD
scheme.

5. Congratulations, you now have a BSD-disklabelled hard disk drive.

 The whole process takes usually about ten minutes, including
double-checking of all the numbers, which is highly recommended. ;-) 

