I've never had an AlphaStation here, so what I say may be wrong but
is worth to try.
skip step 2. and 3. Simply insert the FAT-floppy (fwupdate.exe on it)
into the drive and enter "fwupdate" or "nt".

> My Linux 5.1 Alpha Installation Addendum mentions (as I understand it)
> that to create a boot block device (i.e. the floppy disk), I need to "build
> mboot and
> bootm and push them onto the boot device ...."; This lost me.

This is for loading a kernel and not for updating the BIOS.
This is only required for SRM based kernel-loading, so forget it.

> Are they part of Linux (if so, I'm not even close to doing the
> Linux installation to be able to access these programs)?  It also mentions
> using a program, "RAMWRITE.EXE".  I'm not
> sure what this program is for either.

"RAMWRITE.EXE" aka "RAWRITE.EXE" (a typo, ah ?) is used to transfer
a floppy image (file) onto a floppy. This is a MS-DOG program.
If you have a running Linux system, you can use "dd" instead of it.

> Can anyone please help me figure this out.
> I need to be able to boot (through SRM) the floppy containing the firmware
> update.
> I have an AlphaStation 200 4/233 w/ SRM version 6.1-2 May 8 1996 10:26:42.

SRM on SX and LX board can execute firmware updates without a bootblock
on the floppy. I really think that AlphaStation works the same.
Simply say "NT" or "fwupdate".
I hope it helps.

