On Wed, 22 Jul 1998 Jay.Estabrook@digital.com wrote:

> Ah, do NOT use "make menuconfig" or "make xconfig", they are not reliable
> at this time on the Alphas; use the trusty old "make config"...

Amen to that.

To save heaps of trouble (and bandwidth on axp-list) it would pay to
apply something like the following patch to the next 2.0 release (in
case there will be one). Or at least put a big fat warning with bee
stripes to the README file. Alpha users who are not following axp-list
(or newsgroups) have no way of knowing x/menuconfig are broken.

People please, this ain't rocket science, even a non-kernel-hacker
like me can do this. This is a very bad issue, since at least
with "make xconfig" you can build kernels that *seems* to work for 
a while--and then crash dreadfully. To make it worse, at least
with 2.0.30 I used xconfig with no problems so of course I assumed
it would work with .34 too... (I thought only menuconfig was broken
since it was so obvious, screen got messed up etc.)

--- Makefile	Mon Jul 13 23:47:26 1998
+++ Makefile.pablo	Thu Jul 23 09:47:38 1998
@@ -190,10 +190,18 @@
 	$(CONFIG_SHELL) scripts/Configure -d arch/$(ARCH)/config.in
 
 xconfig: symlinks
+	@if [ $(ARCH) = "alpha" ]; then \
+		echo "xconfig not supported on the $(ARCH) platform."; \
+		echo "Please use 'make config'."; exit 1; \
+	fi
 	$(MAKE) -C scripts kconfig.tk
 	wish -f scripts/kconfig.tk
 
 menuconfig: include/linux/version.h symlinks 
+	@if [ $(ARCH) = "alpha" ]; then \
+		echo "menuconfig not supported on the $(ARCH) platform."; \
+		echo "Please use 'make config'."; exit 1; \
+	fi
 	$(MAKE) -C scripts/lxdialog all
 	$(CONFIG_SHELL) scripts/Menuconfig arch/$(ARCH)/config.in


