>>> Jordan said:
> 
> I have an AlphaStation 255 with a 21064A proc. I have been confronted with
> system names such as, Jensen, Noname, AlphaPC64, and I'm unsure which of
> these is my system. Would any body be able to shed some light on this for
> me ??

Please refer to the Hardware Comnpatibility List for Red Hat 5.1 that RH has
just recently updated (July 7). It is now much more complete than before, I
believe.

Please let me and Red Hat know if there are any inaccuracies, or if the
information is not complete or is confusing in any way.

> What is the best way to install a boot block onto the scsi disk ??

For ARC/AlphaBIOS booting, via MILO in a DOS FS partition, with DOS partition
table (normal fdisk style).

For SRM booting (without use of MILO), the disk must be BSD-style partition
table, with 1024 sectors reserved in fron of the first partition for bootstrap
installation. Use fdisk's "b" (BSD) option, or the separate utility minlabel.

> if I blow milo into the flash, does it overwrite the SRM firmware ??

You MUST NOT, as the AVANTI family is not supported for doing this.

> Is it terribly hard to update the flash's firmware ?? eg changing from SRM
> to AlphaBIOS or ARC or what ever it is, and then back again etc ??

Not hard at all, given the appropriately formatted/configured floppies. Do
you anticipate dual-booting with NT? If so, simply choose AlphaBIOS console
and stick with it. If dual-booting with Digital UNIX, choose SRM console and
stay with that. You only need to switch consoles if you need to boot both DU
and NT.

> Finally when compiling a new kernel, do they have to be specially patched?
> I only ask as I found that the 2.0.35 kernel did not want to compile, even
> when I had set up the architecture specific stuff in Makefile etc.

Yes, unfortunately they still do. Find an already patched source tree on
gatekeeper.dec.com:

	/pub/DEC/Linux-Alpha/Kernels/alpha-sources-2.0.34-0.1.tar.gz

--Jay++

