> I agree, but I am not a kernel programmer.  If there is a problem, it
> really needs to be fixed.  But from where I am standing, I feel like I
> can do nothing about the problem, except enjoy my system crashing.  I
> wonder what it would take to get someone to look into it?

The folks who know how to investigate this apparently don't have the
problem at all. I've been making suggestions as to how anyone seeing
the problem could isolate it; you could follow them and report back.

Recipe:

1) Increase file-max and inode-max
2) Monitor your system over time to see if:
  2a) some processes have many fd's open, by checking /proc/*
  2b) the actual number of fd's used (/proc/sys/kernel/file-nr)
      steadily increases over time

If a program simply isn't closing its fd's, you will see many
processes in /proc/* with many fd's.

If not, and file-nr keeps on increasing, it's a kernel bug where
the kernel is not reusing fd's.

-- g


